<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberal Party (UK)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liberal Party was one of the two major parties of the United Kingdom from its formation in 1859, out of the Whigs, Radicals and Peelites, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Under William Gladstone and later H. H. Asquith and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> it championed free trade, Irish Home Rule, a wider franchise and social reform. Overtaken by the Labour Party after 1918, it merged into the Liberal Democrats in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk.jpg" alt="Liberal Party (UK)" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dn9ahx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liberal_Party_logo_(pre1988).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew from the Whigs, whose roots lie in the Parliamentarian side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, together with free-trade Peelites and reformist Radicals. The Whigs wanted to reduce the power of the Crown and increase that of Parliament, and the more idealistic among them came to support wider democracy for its own sake. Under Earl Grey they returned to power in 1830 and carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">First Reform Act</a> in 1832. Nonconformist Protestants were a major voting bloc that largely won over the Whigs and Liberals, pressing for an end to church rates and religious tests.</p><p>William Gladstone, prime minister four times between 1868 and 1894, was so closely identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">Liberalism</a> for thirty years that the two were synonymous, and his moral tone and appeal to working-class and lower-middle-class audiences marked the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gladstone's support for Irish Home Rule deeply divided the party. After 1886 it lost most of the Whig peerage and the upper and upper-middle classes, while keeping nonconformists and the Celtic fringe. The Liberal Unionists split away in 1886. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> divided it again between Liberal Imperialists and the Pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a>. Under Henry Campbell-Bannerman the party won a landslide in the 1906 general election with 397 MPs. Together with H. H. Asquith, prime minister from 1908 to 1916, it passed reforms that created a basic welfare state, influenced by the New Liberalism of L. T. Hobhouse and John A. Hobson, which held that the state should remove poverty and ignorance as obstacles to freedom.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> called for conscription, censorship and state controls that Liberals had long opposed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> replaced Asquith in 1916 at the head of a coalition increasingly dominated by Conservatives, and the split between their factions weakened the party. By 1924 it had 40 MPs and 18% of the vote, and Labour absorbed most of its former voters. A three-way split followed in 1931 over the National Government. By the 1950s it had won as few as six seats. A revival began under Jo Grimond, leader from 1956, and the Orpington by-election of 1962. In 1981 it formed the SDP–Liberal Alliance with the Social Democratic Party, which won over a quarter of the vote in 1983 but only 23 seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Liberals and the SDP merged in 1988 as the Social and Liberal Democrats, renamed the Liberal Democrats the following year, and a splinter group reconstituted the Liberal Party in 1989. The party's thinkers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-maynard-keynes">John Maynard Keynes</a> and William Beveridge. It was a coalition with no unified national policy, which made it liable to deep splits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liberal Party (UK)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Liberalism of L. T. Hobhouse and John A. Hobson, which emerged in Britain in about 1900 and held that poverty made freedom impossible without a welfare-oriented state, became the programme of the <b>Liberal Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Liberal Party</b> won; Richard Seddon became prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
